e linen","H4714":"Egypt","H5130":"to sprinkle","H4904":"bed","H4753":"myrrh","H174":"aloe","H7076":"cinnamon","H3212":"to walk","H7301":"to quench","H1730":"beloved","H5704":"till","H1242":"morning","H5965":"to rejoice","H159":"beloved","H369":"nothing","H376":"man","H1980":"went","H7350":"distant","H6872":"bundle","H3701":"money","H3947":"take","H3027":"hand","H3677":"full moon","H935":"come","H5186":"to stretch","H7230":"abundance","H3948":"teaching","H2506":"portion","H8193":"lips","H5080":"to banish","H310":"after","H6597":"suddenly","H7794":"cattle","H413":"to(wards)","H2874":"slaughter","H5914":"anklet","H4148":"discipline","H191":"fool(ish)","H6398":"to cleave","H2671":"arrow","H3516":"liver","H4116":"to hasten","H6833":"bird","H6341":"snare","H3045":"to know","H5315":"soul","H6258":"now","H8085":"hear","H7181":"to listen","H6310":"lip","H408":"not","H7847":"to turn aside","H8582":"to go astray","H5410":"path","H7227":"many","H2491":"killed","H5307":"fall","H6099":"mighty","H2026":"to kill","H7585":"Sheol","H3381":"to go down","H2315":"chamber","H4194":"death"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Words. As dangerous temptations always threaten, the same instructions are frequently inculcated. (Worthington)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Thine. But another’s, ver. 19. Give thy heart to wisdom, that it may be guarded against impure love."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","Lattice. No glass was used, on account of the great heat. (Calmet)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Ones. Not in age, but prudence. (Menochius)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Attire. Covered with a veil, (Genesis xxxviii. 15.) though different from that of honest women. (Calmet) — The wanton are commonly the most gaudy; nullarum fere pretiosior cultus est quam quarum pudor vilis est. (St. Cyprian, de Habitu.) — Prepared, &c. Hebrew, “guarded,” (Calmet) or “subtle of heart.” (Protestants) “who makes the hearts of youths take flight.” (Septuagint) (Canticle of Canticles vi. 4.) (Haydock)"],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Not….quiet. Is not in Hebrew. (Calmet) — “She is loud and stubborn, her feet abide not in her house.” (Protestants) (Haydock) — Chaste women are guarded in their speech, and keep at home. (Menochius)"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Prosperity. Or thy welfare, (Cornelius a Lapide; Tirinus) so great is my love for thee. (Menochius) — Vows. And therefore I have a feast prepared. People might carry home the greatest part of the victim to eat, if they were clean, Leviticus vii. 29. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Cords. For greater ease, instead of boards, (Menochius) or the curtains are hung with precious cords from Egypt. (Calmet)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Aloes. Of Syria, (John xix. 39.) different from ours, Numbers xxxiv. 6. (Calmet)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Inebriated. Protestants, “take our fill of love until the morning: let us solace ourselves with loves.” (Haydock) — This passion is a sort of intoxication. (Menochius)"],["haydock",19,null,"haydock","My. Literally, “the man.” (Haydock) — She speaks thus out of contempt. (Calmet)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","The day. Septuagint, “after many days.” Hebrew, “at the day concealed,” or when the people will dwell under tents, (Chaldean; Calmet) or “at the new moon,” when it does not appear. (Piscator) — He will not return for a long time, so that we need apprehend no danger from him. (Menochius)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Lamb. Protestants, “fool to the correction of the stocks,” (Haydock) or “like a shackle (abs.) for the chastisement of a fool.” (Mont.[Montanus?]) — Interpreters have read different words. (Calmet) — Sinners who have given way to temptations, are as inconsiderate as oxen, or birds which hasten to their own ruin. (Worthington)"],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Her. Solomon gave a melancholy proof of this, as well as David, and Amnon. (Calmet)"],["haydock",27,null,"haydock","Death. There can be no precaution too great, chap. ii. 18. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[22,null,0,"Fragments - Exegetical. Fragments from Commentaries on Various Books of Scripture","Hippolytus Exegetical Fragments",0,235,"The \"cemphus\" is a kind of wild sea-bird, which has so immoderate an impulse to sexual enjoyment, that its eyes seem to fill with blood in coition; and it often blindly falls into snares, or into the hands of men. To this, therefore, he compares the man who gives himself up to the harlot on account of his immoderate lust; or else on account of the insensate folly of the creature, for he, too, pursues his object like one senseless. And they say that this bird is so much pleased with foam, that if one should hold foam in his hand as he sails, it will sit upon his hand. And it also brings forth with pain."],[22,null,1,"13 Ascetic Discourses","Discourse 5 -- Second Discourse on Simplicity",1,523,"And again in his speech Solomon also reprehendeth him that is led after his lust like a foolish person, and who lacketh the knowledge which should fight against his passions. \"He goeth after her like a simple man, and as an ox that goeth to the slaughterer, and as a dog to the fetters, and as a stag into whose liver an arrow hath pierced. …"],[26,null,0,"Fragments - Exegetical. Fragments from Commentaries on Various Books of Scripture","Hippolytus Exegetical Fragments",0,235,"You have seen her mischief. Wait not to admit the rising of lust; for her death is everlasting. And for the rest, by her words, her arguments in sooth, she wounds, and by her sins she kills those who yield to her. For many are the forms of wickedness that lead the foolish down to hell. And the chambers of death mean either its depths or its treasure. How, then, is escape possible?"]],"fau":[["hippolytus-of-rome","Hippolytus of Rome",0,"father",null,0],["philoxenus-of-mabbug","Philoxenus of Mabbug",0,"oriental",null,0]],"ftr":[["Ante-Nicene Fathers, vol. 5 (1886)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/anf05"],["E. A. Wallis Budge, The Discourses of Philoxenus (1894)"]]}